CLAIMS.update({
    'case-walk': {
        'text': "The case is told as a walk: the order of its stops, and the hour at each of the four on the night itself (21:28 to 21:40 on 8 October 1871), are chosen for the telling, from the hours the witnesses gave (Schaffer 9:28; Sullivan about half past nine, or 'about nine o'clock' in 1884; the Board 9:20-9:25; Andreas a quarter to nine). The later stops (the inquiry, the legend, the later theories, the City Council) are narration over the place, and carry no date.",
        'confidence': 'reconstructed', 'liberty': CASE, 'sources': []},
    'sullivan-affidavit': att(
        "Dennis Sullivan's affidavit of 15 October 1871, printed by Andreas: 'he was at Patrick O'Leary's house, No. 137 DeKoven Street, on Sunday night, the 8th of October, 1871, from about 8:30 to 9 o'clock at night, during which time Mr. O'Leary and wife were in bed; that he went a few lots east of O'Leary's, on the opposite side of DeKoven Street, until about half-past nine o'clock, when he saw the fire. He went across the street, and cried \"Fire, fire,\" and went into O'Leary's barn, where he found the hay in the loft on fire. He then attempted to cut loose the horse and cows, but failed to save anything but a half-burned calf.' Andreas's note: 'Mr. Sullivan's statement before the commissioners places the time at about 9:30. He now says \"about nine o'clock.\"' The Board names him 'a drayman by the name of Daniel [Dennis] Sullivan'.",
        [A], "Andreas vol. II, 'The Burning of Chicago', pp. 708-709, the affidavits taken by Michael McDermott and the footnote on Sullivan's time; the report's first paragraph."),
    'peg-leg': att(
        "The Tribune, 6 October 1997: the City Council's Committee on Police and Fire voted to exonerate Catherine O'Leary, and 'the actions of Daniel \"Peg Leg\" Sullivan, who had implicated Mrs. O'Leary in the Oct. 8, 1871 fire, should be scrutinized.' The Tribune, 3 March 1998: the City Council's pardon 'will do little more than shift the mantle of guilt from a poor, immigrant Irish woman to a poor, immigrant (as well as disabled) Irish man, Daniel \"Peg Leg\" Sullivan'; 'amateur historian Dick Bales, a Wheaton attorney' (the Tribune, 8 October 1997), writing in the Spring 1997 journal of the Illinois State Historical Society, compared land records of 1871 with his testimony and 'theorized Sullivan could not, as he had testified, have been sitting two houses down when he initially spotted the blaze – another house would have blocked his view. Nor could a man with a wooden leg have hobbled the 193 feet to the O'Leary barn in the short time he said he had.' Bales concluded 'that Peg Leg had actually started the fire himself, perhaps while feeding his own family cow on the O'Learys' hay, and lied to cover it up.'",
        [T97, T97B, T98], "Tribune 1997-10-06 (Steve Mills), paragraph 3; Tribune 1997-10-08, paragraph 4; Tribune 1998-03-03, paragraphs 3-5."),
    'oleary-testimony': att(
        "Catherine O'Leary before the Board of Police and Fire Commissioners, November 1871: 'I was in bed myself and my husband and five children when this fire commenced.' Asked how it caught: 'I could not tell anything of the fire only that two men came by the door. I guess it was my husband got outside the door and he ran back to the bedroom and said \"Kate the barn is afire.\" I ran out and the whole barn was on fire.' On the party in the front rooms: 'There was a little music there.' 'Her husband was a fiddler.' The time: 'As near as I can guess it was a little after nine o'clock.' What she had heard: 'There was one out of the party went in for to milk my cows.' From whom: 'Mrs. White.' Why milk: 'Some said it was for oysters. I could not tell anything only what I heard from the outside.' Mrs. McLaughlin 'said she never was in the stable.' Insurance: 'Never had five cents insurance' ... 'and upon my word I worked so hard for them.' The museum: the Board 'interviewed some fifty individuals. Catherine O'Leary was naturally a star witness'.",
        [OL], "O'Leary Legend Library, '“Kate the barn is afire”', the introduction and her answers in order."),
    'rogan': att(
        "Andreas, condensing the inquiry: 'Dennis Rogan, of No. 112 DeKoven Street, testified that he was at the O'Leary house about half past eight Sunday evening. Mr. and Mrs. O'Leary were in bed. Rogan inquired why they had gone to bed so early, and Mrs. O'Leary replied that it was because she had a \"sore foot.\"' He heard music at McLaughlin's 'at the time of the fire', and thought it was fifteen minutes after the fire was discovered before the engines arrived. Patrick O'Leary's affidavit says they were 'awakened by Dennis Rogan, of No. 112 DeKoven'. Andreas: it 'does not seem at all probable that Mrs. O'Leary, who was then suffering from a wounded foot, would leave her bed and go into the barn at nine o'clock to do work which she was accustomed to do at an earlier hour.'",
        [A], "Andreas vol. II, p. 709, the testimony of Dennis Rogan and the affidavit of Patrick O'Leary; p. 708, the paragraph beginning 'It is possible that some petty pilferer'."),
    'mclaughlin': att(
        "Andreas, condensing the inquiry: 'Catharine McLaughlin, who lived in the front part of O'Leary's house, testified that no member of her family went to the barn that night to obtain milk for punch. She knew nothing about the origin of the fire. Some young people were celebrating the arrival of a cousin from Ireland that evening, but all were in the house when the fire broke out.' Andreas sums up the testimony: the McLaughlins' party 'indulged in the use of beer, but had no whisky or milk punch', and 'none of the McLaughlins visited the barn that evening for the purpose of obtaining milk'. 'It is possible that some petty pilferer entered the barn; it is possible that some member of the McLaughlin family did attempt to procure milk, and met with an accident, causing the fire'.",
        [A], "Andreas vol. II, pp. 708-709, the paragraph beginning 'That Dennis Sullivan discovered the fire' and the testimony of Catharine McLaughlin."),
    'box-342': att(
        "The Board's report: 'The first information received by the Fire Department came from the alarm struck in the fire-alarm office at 9:30. The alarm sounded Box No. 342, at the corner of Canalport Avenue and Halsted Street, a point in the direction of the fire, but a mile beyond it.' 'There was no signal given by any box to the central office, but the box was given by Mathias Schaffer, from the Court house cupola'. 'If any person set the fire, either by accident or design, he was careful not to give the alarm.' Andreas: Schaffer said 'it was 9:28 p.m., when he first discovered the light of the fire'; 'the error in judgment which located the fire, after it had been going about forty-three minutes, a mile beyond its actual site, was a fatal mistake.' The still alarm sent to engine No. 6 'was about seventeen minutes earlier than the first public alarm from the Court-house tower.'",
        [OL, A], "Museum, O'Leary Legend Library, 'The Official Report', second paragraph; Andreas vol. II pp. 709-711, the paragraphs beginning 'The Press, and the earlier writers' and 'This statement would show', and Schaffer's statement."),
    'board-finding': att(
        "The Board of Police and Fire Commissioners' report: 'The Board find that the fire originated in a two-story barn in the rear of No. 137 DeKoven Street, the premises being owned by Patrick O'Leary.' 'There is no proof that any person had been in the barn after nightfall that evening. Whether it originated from a spark blown from a chimney on that windy night, or was set on fire by human agency, we are unable to determine. Mr. O'Leary and all his family prove to have been in bed and asleep at the time. There was a small party in the front part of O'Leary's house, which was occupied by Mr. McLaughlin and wife. But we fail to find any evidence that anybody from McLaughlin's part of the house went near the barn that night.' The museum: 'The report as a whole was criticized at the time, not for clearing Mrs. O'Leary but for covering up incompetence, and perhaps even some corruption, in the department.' Andreas: the inquiry 'was begun November 23, 1871, under the direction of the Board of Police and Fire Commissioners'; 'Some fifty witnesses were interrogated'; 'the investigation lasted several days, and naturally produced a mass of irrelevant matter'; 'The report appears to be little more than an attempt to quiet the too loud expressions of dissatisfaction with the Department.'",
        [OL, A], "Museum, O'Leary Legend Library, 'The Official Report', the introduction and first paragraph; Andreas vol. II pp. 708-711, the paragraphs beginning 'But even the existence', 'The official inquiry into the origin' and 'The foregoing report states'."),
    'ahern': att(
        "The museum: 'on the fortieth anniversary of the great conflagration a reporter named Michael Ahern, who was working for the Chicago Republican at the time of the fire, boasted in the Tribune that he and two now-deceased cronies made the whole thing up.' 'Ahern opined that one of the revelers went out to get milk and ended up burning Chicago down.' 'after Ahern's revelation appeared a long-time colleague stated that he had ghost-written the Tribune story under Ahern's byline. As for Ahern himself, this other reporter confided, \"The booze got him many years ago, and he has not been able to do any newspaper work.\"' Wikipedia gives the year differently: 'In 1893 the reporter Michael Ahern retracted the \"cow-and-lantern\" story, admitting it was fabricated'.",
        [OL, WP], "Museum, 'The O'Leary Legend', the paragraph beginning 'On top of this'; Wikipedia, 'Great Chicago Fire', section 'Origin'."),
    'scapegoat': att(
        "The museum: 'Most of the many contemporary depictions of the origin of the fire presented a comic caricature of Catherine O'Leary as a hapless figure.' She 'was a familiar and recognizable type who could readily be made to stand for careless building, sloppy conduct, and a shiftless immigrant underclass. Blaming her adapted existing anti-Catholic, anti-immigrant, and possibly anti-female sentiments to the terrible calamity'. 'As a poor clumsy Irishwoman and not a sworn enemy of the social order, she was a disempowered comic stereotype'. Painters and cartoonists named the cow Daisy; Norman Rockwell painted 'Mrs. Catherine O'Leary Milking Daisy' about 1935. 'It is highly unlikely that we will ever have definitive knowledge of the cause of the fire.'",
        [OL], "'The O'Leary Legend', the essay's paragraphs beginning 'But Mrs. O'Leary offered' and 'It is highly unlikely'; the galleries 'A Star is Born' (ichi-02945) and the next one (ichi-64474)."),
    'cohn': att(
        "The Tribune, 8 October 1997: Louis M. Cohn's claim 'first surfaced Sept. 28, 1944 when his $35,000 estate, bequeathed in his name to Northwestern University's Medill School of Journalism, was ceremoniously handed over'. The university's press release: 'He asserted that he and Mrs. O'Leary's son, in the company of several other boys, were shooting dice in the hayloft . . . by the light of a lantern, when one of the boys accidently overturned the lantern, thus setting the barn afire. Mr. Cohn never denied that when the other boys fled, he stopped long enough to scoop up the money.' 'He was born March 10, 1853 in Breslau, Prussia'; he 'died in 1942'. 'Cohn's recorded nine-page will, signed five months before his death, contains no reference to the fire.' Everything else said about the origin, 'the cow, the comet, and most recently, Daniel “Peg Leg” Sullivan', is, says the Northwestern professor Carl Smith, 'interesting, but ultimately unprovable theory'. Wikipedia credits the Tribune's articles of 8 October 1997 and 3 March 1998 to Anthony DeBartolo.",
        [T97B, T98, WP], "Tribune 1997-10-08, paragraphs 2, 7-8, 11-13, 25; Tribune 1998-03-03 on the same release; Wikipedia, 'Great Chicago Fire', section 'Origin' (the DeBartolo citations)."),
    'meteor': att(
        "The museum lists, among the theories since 1871: 'A meteor split into pieces as it fell to earth October 8, setting off the simultaneous catastrophic fires in Chicago and Peshtigo, Wisconsin, plus a lesser conflagration in Michigan.' Wikipedia: the idea goes back to 1882, to Ignatius L. Donnelly's Ragnarok, and was revived in 2004 by the engineer and physicist Robert Wood, who tied it to Biela's Comet; small meteorites 'are cool to the touch after reaching the ground, so this theory has not found favor in the scientific community.'",
        [OL, WP], "Museum, 'The O'Leary Legend', the paragraph beginning 'Several additional theories'; Wikipedia, 'Great Chicago Fire', section 'Origin', the comet paragraph."),
    'exoneration': att(
        "The museum: 'At the hundredth anniversary of the fire in 1971, the Chicago Association of Commerce and Industry absolved Mrs. O'Leary and pointed the finger of blame ... at Daniel Sullivan'. 'In October of 1997, the City Council committee on the police and fire departments held a somewhat tongue-in-cheek hearing that culminated in the official exoneration of Catherine O'Leary. The hearing was held at the Chicago Fire Academy, which stands on the site where the O'Leary cottage was located.' The Tribune, 6 October 1997: Ald. William Beavers and Edward Burke decided 'that O'Leary and her cow had been unfairly maligned'; Nancy Knight Connolly, her great-great-granddaughter: 'We always knew that she was innocent'. The Tribune, 3 March 1998: 'Ald. Edward M. Burke ushered through the City Council a resolution exonerating Catherine O'Leary and her cow of all blame'.",
        [OL, T97, T98], "Museum, gallery following 'A Star is Born', the caption to McCutcheon's cartoon of 1934; Tribune 1997-10-06 paragraphs 1-2 and 4; Tribune 1998-03-03 paragraph 1."),
    'fire-academy': att(
        "The museum: 'The Chicago Fire Academy, whose footprint includes the site of the O'Leary Cottage, was dedicated on May 15, 1961. Egon Weiner's representation of a flame stands just to the west of the entrance at Jefferson and DeKoven.' 'By the 1880s the O'Leary cottage was gone'.",
        [OL], "Gallery following 'A Star is Born', the captions 'Full Circle' and 'Sacred Site'."),
})
